Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Fatigue/pruritus/jaundice, Association with ulcerative colitis, Recurrent ascending cholangitis, Beaded bile ducts on MRCP

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-054
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of uncontrollable shivering, high fever, and a dull ache in the upper right abdomen in a patient with a known history of ulcerative colitis.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted high fever, rigors, and right upper quadrant discomfort in a patient with inflammatory bowel disease, representing a classic presentation of acute ascending cholangitis secondary to primary sclerosing cholangitis. This is a life-threatening septic emergency. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was absolutely essential to confirm clinical signs of severe sepsis and immediately facilitate emergency ambulance transfer for intravenous antibiotics.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am freezing and shaking uncontrollably, I have a deep ache under my ribs, and my spouse says my eyes look yellow."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden onset of high fever and intense rigors (uncontrollable shivering) commencing 3 hours ago. Accompanied by right upper quadrant abdominal pain and discomfort. Over the past month, the patient has noted progressive fatigue, systemic lethargy, intermittent generalized pruritus, and fluctuating mild jaundice. Reports unintentional weight loss and steatorrhoea. Strong clinical association present: patient has a known 10-year history of ulcerative colitis. This represents a recurrent episode, having experienced a similar, milder attack of ascending cholangitis 6 months ago.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively shivering, unable to walk unassisted.
Social/Safety Concerns: Rapid clinical deterioration; high risk of septic shock.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 122 | Temp: 39.4 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient is acutely unwell, toxic-appearing, diaphoretic, and experiencing active rigors.
Physical Exam: Scleral icterus and visible jaundice of the skin are present. Abdominal examination reveals localized right upper quadrant tenderness and a palpable, tender liver edge (hepatomegaly). Mild splenomegaly is also noted. Tachycardia and tachypnoea are present, indicating severe systemic sepsis. The clinical presentation definitively demonstrates fatigue/pruritus/jaundice, an association with ulcerative colitis, and recurrent ascending cholangitis; characteristic 'beaded' bile ducts are presumed pending urgent inpatient MRCP.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin at bedside.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with mesalazine for UC. Took paracetamol 2 hours ago with no reduction in fever.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Ascending Cholangitis (Secondary to suspected Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis) - Status: Exacerbated (Septic)
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent presence of frank jaundice, severe rigors, and the strong epidemiological link to ulcerative colitis, which overwhelmingly points to a primary ductal pathology (PSC) rather than isolated gallbladder inflammation.

Autoimmune Hepatitis Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyperacute onset of high fevers and rigors, which defines a bacterial infectious process (cholangitis) superimposed on the chronic stricturing disease, rather than a sterile autoimmune flare.

Ulcerative Colitis Flare (Toxic Megacolon) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symptoms being localized to the biliary tree (jaundice, RUQ pain) rather than profound lower abdominal cramping and massive bloody diarrhoea.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is critically high)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an unequivocal clinical picture of acute ascending cholangitis (Charcot's triad: fever, jaundice, RUQ pain) superimposed on the classic demographic and historical features of Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (ulcerative colitis, chronic cholestatic symptoms). The objective presence of significant tachycardia, tachypnoea, and hypotension firmly indicates advancing biliary sepsis. This condition carries a high mortality rate if left untreated. An urgent out-of-hours home visit was absolutely necessary to definitively recognize the septic state and initiate immediate emergency medical transport for life-saving intravenous antibiotics and urgent biliary imaging/decompression.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately contacted emergency services (000) for priority ambulance dispatch. Administered IM Ceftriaxone 1g (as per local emergency protocols for acute sepsis pending transport).
Positive Behavioral Change: Remain completely flat in bed while awaiting the ambulance. Keeping the head low physically maintains blood flow to the brain and major organs, preventing a dangerous drop in blood pressure caused by the severe infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently awaiting ambulance transport. If the patient loses consciousness, becomes acutely confused, or stops breathing before paramedics arrive, the spouse must commence basic life support if trained and update 000 dispatch immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate hospital intervention.
Clinical Course: Without urgent intravenous antibiotics, this infection will rapidly cause septic shock and multi-organ failure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to drive the patient to the hospital; wait for the ambulance. Paramedics can provide life-saving intravenous fluids and blood pressure support en route if required.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A chronic condition linked to the ulcerative colitis has caused the bile ducts inside the liver to become heavily scarred and narrowed. One of these narrowed areas has become completely blocked and infected with bacteria. The bacteria are now entering the bloodstream, causing the severe shaking and fevers. Urgent hospital care is needed to kill the bacteria with strong IV antibiotics and potentially use a camera to unblock the duct.
Actionable Steps: Do not eat or drink anything. The surgical team will need the stomach to be completely empty to safely perform any necessary procedures to remove the blockage.

